15-        Nowadays, the use of spices and other herbs have extensively increased due to their medicinal
16-        values all over the world. However, the monitoring of heavy metal contamination in spices has
17-        also been increased in recent years. A study has been conducted to determine the concentration
18:        of eight heavy metals such as Lead (Pb), Cadmium (Cd), Chromium (Cr), Arsenic (As), Zinc
19-        (Zn), Iron (Fe), Copper (Cu) and Manganese (Mn) in mainly three different types of turmeric
20-        samples (Curcuma longa). The turmeric samples analyzed were (i) Unpacked bulk powder
21-        available at three different local markets in Dhaka (ii) Packed and marketed by three branded
22-        companies, and (iii) Raw turmeric directly collected from different local farmlands. The
23-        samples were analyzed using Atomic Absorption Spectroscopy (AAS). The concentration of
24-        most of the heavy metals were found within the limit recommended by WHO (6.0000 ppm)
25:        except lead (12.3469 ppm) in unpacked-3 bulk sample. This study also showed the
26-        concentration of heavy metals were present in a lower amount in packed power samples
27-        compared to unpacked bulk powder samples. The result of this analysis would be helpful for
28-        public awareness in consumption of different spices. The concerned authorities who are
29-        responsible for monitoring and regulating the food chain in the market in our country may also
30-        get a great message about heavy metal contamination of spices.
31-
32-Key words: Commercial turmeric powder, Heavy metal, Toxicity.
33-
34-
35-INTRODUCTION
36-Spices are different parts of the plant like leaves, roots, fruits, flowers, or stems used as
37-coloring, flavoring or preserving agent in food (Hirasa & Takemasu, 1998). There are
--
111-
112-Standard solution preparation: Standard solutions were prepared from stock solutions
113-for the respective metal (Fe-1000, Mn-1000, Cu-1000, Zn-1000, Cr-1000, Ar-1000, Pb-
114:1000, Cd-1000; Kanto Chemical Co., Japan) in order to find the calibration curve.
115-
116-Method: Concentration of the heavy metals (Lead, Cadmium, Chromium, Arsenic, Zinc,
117-Iron, Copper, Manganese) in eight standard solutions and in nine different turmeric spice
118-sample solutions were determined (Zeng, 2004) using Atomic Absorption Spectroscopy
119-(AA-7000, Shimadju). Blank samples were made using only the reagents i.e. without the
120-sample in order to eliminate any background concentration of metals in the entire study.
121-
